{"name":"Zentai suit","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Woman_posing_in_shimmery_zentai.jpg/960px-Woman_posing_in_shimmery_zentai.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman_posing_in_shimmery_zentai.jpg","caption":"Woman posing in shimmery zentai","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A zentai is a stretch fabric suit, usually nylon-spandex, that covers the whole body. The name comes from the Japanese zenshin taitsu, full-body tights.[1]","It is used for costumes, dance and fan culture, video chroma-key work and anonymity: the suit hides the wearer's face and body detail, so appearance is replaced by a uniform shape or colour.[1][2]"],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Wearers pull on a one-piece or multi-piece suit in a solid colour or pattern. Uses include sports-fan costumes, dance groups, performance art and green-screen effects.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Face-covering means some jurisdictions restrict wearing them in public; France may fine up to 150 euros, and Major League Baseball bans the hoods. Heat build-up and limited vision are practical concerns.[1]"],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Woman_posing_in_shimmery_zentai.jpg/960px-Woman_posing_in_shimmery_zentai.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Woman_posing_in_shimmery_zentai.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Woman posing in shimmery zentai","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","creator":"Marcy_Anarchy","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman_posing_in_shimmery_zentai.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Female_mannequi